<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kodungallur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cranganore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kodungallur is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrissur">Thrissur</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, near the mouth of the Periyar river on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Known to Europeans as Cranganore, it lies near the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram">Mahodayapuram</a>, capital of the Chera Perumals from the 9th to the 12th century, and many scholars locate the ancient Indo-Roman port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a> nearby. Tradition makes it the landing place of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle">Thomas the Apostle</a> and the home of early Jewish and Muslim communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kodungallur.jpg" alt="Kodungallur" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wouter Schouten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cranganore_in_Cranganore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kodungallur lies on the banks of the Periyar on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>, 36 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> and 38 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrissur">Thrissur</a>. As a port at the northern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> lagoons, it was a strategic entry point to the backwaters. It has had many names, among them Cranganore (anglicised) and Cranganor in Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram">Mahodayapuram</a>, Shingly and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanchi">Vanchi</a>. Several of the foreign forms, such as Shinkali and Jangli, derive from the name of the River Changala, a tributary of the Periyar. Scholars believe that the ancient harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a>, at the mouth of the Periyar, coincides with modern Kodungallur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In early historic times the harbour was visited by navigators from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> traded continuously with the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and sailors acquired black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, pearls, muslin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, diamonds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and perfumes from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. Traditions of several faiths attach to the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-christians">Saint Thomas Christians</a> hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle">Thomas the Apostle</a> landed here in the middle of the first century CE and founded seven churches. According to Kerala Muslim tradition the first mosque in India was built here in 624 CE with the consent of the last Cheraman Perumal.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the town was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram">Mahodayapuram</a>, seat of the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> rulers sacked the port in the 11th century, and after the Chera Perumal rule ended in the early 12th century Kodungallur became a principality allied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>. A flood or earthquake in 1341 is postulated to have silted the river's harbour so that large vessels could scarcely enter, and trade moved to Cochin and Calicut.</p><p>On 1 September 1504 the Portuguese under Suarez de Menezes almost completely destroyed the port, and in October of that year Lopo Soares attacked it again with a combined force of about 1,000 Portuguese and 1,000 soldiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>, which captured and burned the city. Reports say the Saint Thomas Christian quarters were spared. The Portuguese built Fort Cranganore in 1523 and enlarged it in 1565. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took it in 1663, and on 31 July 1789 they handed their holdings in Kodungallur to Travancore for 300,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> silver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kodungallur is a municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrissur">Thrissur</a> district, headquarters of its own tehsil. The Thiruvanchikulam Mahadeva Temple, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, was the patron shrine of the Chera Perumals and is still the family deity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> royal family. The Kodungallur Bhagavathy Temple to the goddess Bhadrakali heads 64 Bhadrakali temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. The Muziris Heritage Project, launched by the government of Kerala in 2006, works to recover and preserve the heritage of the region from North Paravur to Kodungallur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kodungallur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its capital was Mahodayapuram, also called Makotai, near modern <b>Kodungallur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/chera-perumals-of-makotai">Chera Perumals of Makotai</a></li><li><blockquote>The Malabar posts were organised as a commandment governed from Fort Kochi, with factories at Cannanore, <b>Cranganore</b> and Quilon.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-malabar">Dutch Malabar</a></li></ul></section>
